//! Digital signals.

import std::sources::Rail

/// A digital line together with the rail that defines its logic levels. The line swings
/// between the rail's `neg` and `pos`.
///
/// The four levels are voltages relative to the rail's `neg`, as a part's datasheet gives
/// them, and a part states those that it has. A connection between two lines does not
/// equate them: each belongs to its own pin (ADR 0046). Equating them would make every driver and
/// receiver contradict each other. The connection does join their reference rails, so a driver
/// and a receiver on different supplies are joined line to line.
///
/// - `vih`: the lowest voltage an input reads as high.
/// - `vil`: the highest voltage an input reads as low.
/// - `voh`: the lowest voltage an output drives a high to, at the load the datasheet names.
/// - `vol`: the highest voltage an output drives a low to, at the load the datasheet names.
///
/// After the rails are solved, the solver requires of each output on a net that its `voh`
/// is always at least the `vih` of every input there, and its `vol` at most their `vil`; it
/// says "not evaluated" when a level has no value or the two grounds are not the same net.
/// The pin `.line` has `.abs_min` and `.abs_max`, which a part states in the pin row of
/// `.line` (`.a.line input "A" "2" abs_min -0.5V abs_max 6.5V`, ADR 0126) and the solver
/// checks against the worst-case voltage of its net in the same way.
///
/// `.swing` is the range of voltage the line can have, relative to the rail's `neg` (the
/// line's ground, as the four levels are), for a part whose output swings to a supply that
/// has no pad (a module's internal 1.8 V rail): `.swing &= 0V..1.8V`. A signed range is read as
/// it is written, so `-0.3V..1.8V` goes 0.3 V below the ground. A line that states a swing is
/// held to it, when the solver judges a receiver's absolute maximum on the net that it drives
/// and bounds a resistor's current; a line that states none swings from the ground to the top
/// of its reference rail's voltage, as before (ADR 0081); with no `voh` or `vol` stated its unloaded
/// levels are the swing's top and bottom (amendment 1). It is no load.
///
/// Why a line states its swing (ADR 0081, issue 220). A module whose outputs swing to an internal
/// supply that has no pad (a cellular module's STATUS and UART TX pins swing to an internal 1.8 V
/// rail) could not state its levels: a `Logic` needs a `reference` rail, and a rail in a part
/// needs pads, so the part could not state the rail without inventing pins. Left as plain pins,
/// the receiver's absolute-maximum check said `not evaluated`, and a 1.8 V line into a pin that
/// survives 1.0 V passed in silence. The swing is a member of an interface in `std`, so there is
/// no new syntax, and it states nothing until a part says it. The ground still comes from the
/// reference rail: a part with no pad for its internal supply joins `.reference` to a supply it
/// does have (`.status.reference ~ .vbat`), or to its ground rail, for the ground only; the swing
/// says what the line swings to. What reads it: the absolute-maximum check (`net_range` in
/// `crates/netsmith-solve/src/levels.rs`), where a driver that states a swing contributes exactly
/// that range to the worst-case voltage of its net, and the Ohm's-law bound on a resistor's
/// current (`logic_swing` in `ohm.rs`). It is not a level, and does not make a pair of lines
/// "stated" for the levels check, so a pair is still looked at only when one of them states one
/// of the four. A derived level is compared as a range of one value, so levels read as fractions
/// of a rail (ADR 0082) are not derived from a swing. The open-drain rule of ADR 0075 is
/// unchanged: a line that states a low and no high takes its high from its pull-up, and only when
/// there is none does the swing's top stand in.
///
/// What was not taken:
///
/// - A rail with no pad inside a part. It is a new kind of declaration (a rail that is not
///   placed), with consequences in lowering, the pin tables and the net rules, and it states a
///   voltage when only an excursion of one line is wanted.
/// - A part that states an absolute maximum on a driven net. A net belongs to the board, not to a
///   part, and the check would need a second place that sets a net's range.
/// - A swing relative to a rail's `pos`. A part with no pad has no `pos`, and a range relative to
///   the ground is what the datasheet's table gives.
/// - `optional` on the member (ADR 0074). No relation reads the member, so nothing is skipped; the
///   solver reads whether it has a value.
/// - A derived `voh` of `swing.lo` for the worst case: the swing's range holds the lowest and the
///   highest of its ends, and a part that knows the high it reaches states `voh`. And a derived
///   level that counts as stated, which would make a swing alone start the check of every pair on
///   the net.
///
/// ADR 0081 first left open whether `voh` should default to the swing's high; its amendment 1
/// answered it (the owner approved the derivation, 2026-11-08). Accepted as built, 2026-11-11.
///
/// Three members are `optional` (ADR 0074) and a part states those its datasheet gives, for the
/// DC analysis (ADR 0076, amendment 8): `.ii`, the most current the pin takes or gives when it
/// is high impedance, at any voltage from its ground to its supply (`.ii &= 1uA`: the datasheet's
/// `Ii`, which is a magnitude); and `.roh` and `.rol`, the output resistance when the pin drives
/// high and when it drives low (`.rol &= 20ohm..50ohm`), which are used in place of the ones the
/// levels' test currents give (`.voh &= .vdd.voltage - 0.4V at 2mA`) when a part states them.
interface Logic {
    .line: Pin
    .reference: Rail
    .swing: Voltage
    .vih: Voltage
    .vil: Voltage
    .voh: Voltage
    .vol: Voltage
    .ii: Current optional
    .roh: Resistance optional
    .rol: Resistance optional
}

/// The logic levels of all the lines of a bus interface, as a datasheet gives them for a
/// supply: the four voltages of `Logic` and the rail they are relative to. A bus interface
/// (`std::bus::Spi`, `Uart`, `I2c`, `Swd`) has one as `.levels`, so a part that has a bus states
/// them once for the interface: `.spi.levels.reference ~ .vdd`, `.spi.levels.vih &= 0.7 *
/// .vdd.voltage`, and an input's limit in its pin row (`.spi.sclk input "SCK" "1" abs_max
/// .vdd.voltage + 0.3V`, ADR 0126). The solver judges
/// a bus pin as it judges the `.line` of a `Logic`, with the numbers of ADR 0046; which lines
/// a part drives and which it reads is what its pin table says. `~` between two bus interfaces
/// joins no levels (each belongs to its own part, and the reference rail is that part's
/// supply), and a part that states none of them is not judged.
///
/// `.swing` is the range of voltage the bus's lines can have relative to the reference rail's
/// `neg`, as in `Logic` (ADR 0081).
///
/// The reference rail is a reference for the checker and not a load: a part that joins it to
/// its supply (`.spi.levels.reference ~ .vdd`) gives the net a rail whose current nobody could
/// state, so `Levels` states it, `.reference.current &= 0A`, and the part's draw is all in its
/// supply's `current`. The optional members `.ii`, `.roh` and `.rol` are those of `Logic`.
interface Levels {
    .reference: Rail
    .reference.current &= 0A
    .swing: Voltage
    .vih: Voltage
    .vil: Voltage
    .voh: Voltage
    .vol: Voltage
    .ii: Current optional
    .roh: Resistance optional
    .rol: Resistance optional
}
